# TABLEWARE LEAD CRYSTAL (extra-light lead flint). Schott LLF1 catalog Sellmeier.
# aliases: tableware-crystal
#
# The glass to match a real decanter or wine glass. To be sold as "lead crystal" in the EU
# a glass must hold at least 24% PbO and reach n_D >= 1.545 (Directive 69/493/EEC, as cited
# by Hynes & Jonson, Chem. Soc. Rev. 1997), and LLF1 is the catalogued lead flint at that
# point of the lead-silicate line. Measured from these coefficients, in the columns of
# crystal.glass:
#
#     glass              n(486)   n(589)   n(656)   n_F-n_C    V_d    f/R of a solid sphere
#     BK7                1.5224   1.5167   1.5143   0.00806   64.2        1.468
#     tableware (LLF1)   1.5566   1.5481   1.5446   0.01198   45.7        1.412
#     crystal (F2)       1.6321   1.6199   1.6150   0.01706   36.3        1.307
#
# So glassware splays a caustic 1.5x as far across the spectrum as BK7, against F2's 2.1x:
# its fire is real but quieter. It also reflects less (4.6% at normal incidence, F2 5.6%)
# and traps less, with light total-internally-reflecting only past 40.2 deg (F2: 38.1 deg).
# Use `glass:crystal` (F2) instead when the point is colour thrown on a screen.
form sellmeier
B 1.21640125 0.13366454 0.883399468
C 0.00857807248 0.0420143003 107.59306
